<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Palatinate-Sulzbach</h1><p class="st-meta">1656 CE – 1742 CE · capital Sulzbach</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Wittelsbach duchy in the Upper Palatinate, a centre of religious tolerance and Hebrew printing whose line inherited the Palatinate and Bavaria.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sulzbach was split from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a> in 1614 as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> for Augustus, and in 1656 his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-august-count-palatine-of-sulzbach">Christian August</a> gained full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over it as a duchy. Christian August converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1655 but kept his Protestant subjects' rights.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-august-count-palatine-of-sulzbach">Christian August</a> introduced the simultaneum, letting Catholics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutherans</a> share churches, and allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to settle. Sulzbach became a centre of learning: the Christian Kabbalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-knorr-von-rosenroth">Christian Knorr von Rosenroth</a> served at court, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> presses flourished from 1669. The duchy's economy relied on iron ore and smelting in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/upper-palatinate">Upper Palatinate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-theodore-elector-of-bavaria">Charles Theodore</a> of Sulzbach inherited the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate</a> in 1742, merging Sulzbach into it. In 1777 he also inherited the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>, and the Sulzbach line thus united the Wittelsbach lands.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/palatinate-sulzbach.jpg" alt="Palatinate-Sulzbach" width="320" height="232"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Pfalz-Sulzbach_(1789)_-_DE.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1656 CE – 1742 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sulzbach</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christian August, Theodore Eustace, Charles Theodore</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pfalz-Sulzbach</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1699&amp;focus=palatinate-sulzbach" aria-label="Palatinate-Sulzbach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4950 453 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Palatinate-Sulzbach lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5270 709L5259 732M5270 709L5200 712"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5259 732h0M5200 712h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1699&amp;focus=palatinate-sulzbach">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
